<h1>Text and Data Mining Policy</h1>
<p class="meta">In force &middot; Version 1.2 &middot; 2026-08-09 &middot; Juan Lentino &middot; juanlentino.com</p>

<div class="flag flag-live" role="note">
<strong>In force — self-drafted, not legal advice</strong>
<p>These terms are in force. They are written by the rightsholder rather
than by a lawyer, and they lean deliberately on a standard public licence clause &mdash;
<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode#s3a">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International, section 3(a)</a> &mdash; for the definition of
attribution, rather than inventing one. Nothing here is legal advice. Corrections and questions to
<a href="https://juanlentino.com/contact/">https://juanlentino.com/contact/</a>.</p>
</div>

<p>This page states how this site licenses text and data mining, AI training, and retrieval access
to its content. It is the human-readable document that the site's machine-readable rights signals
point at: the <code>TDM-Policy</code> header on every response, the
<code>tdm-policy</code> field in <a href="/.well-known/tdmrep.json">tdmrep.json</a>, and the
attribution standard named in <a href="https://juanlentino.com/license.xml">license.xml</a>.</p>

<p><strong>The short version.</strong> Search and AI-assisted retrieval are free and unconditional.
Training is reserved by default, and licensed to anyone who attributes — in the output, by name and
canonical URL. The conditions are in <a href="#grant">&sect;2</a> and are written to be checkable
from the outside.</p>

